\paragraph{Dose-response analysis.}
To characterize the marginal value of additional trials, we extend both Retry and Reflexion to $N{=}5$ trials on the 10 death-prone tasks ($n{=}200$ per condition; Figure~\ref{fig:dose-response}). Retry is nearly flat: death rate decreases from $48.0\%$ ($N{=}1$) to $43.5\%$ ($N{=}5$), a $4.5$~pp reduction across five trials. Reflexion shows a qualitatively different pattern: a steep initial decline from $47.5\%$ ($N{=}1$) to $34.0\%$ ($N{=}3$, $\Delta = -13.5$~pp, $z = 2.86$, $p < 0.005$), followed by a plateau at $34$--$36.5\%$ for $N \geq 3$ (pairwise $z$-tests $p > 0.5$, Wilson CIs overlapping). That Reflexion at $N{=}3$ ($34.0\%$) converges to nearly the same floor as $10{\times}$ capability scaling ($34.5\%$, 72B) suggests a shared structural bound: both interventions attenuate death but cannot close the ${\sim}$18~pp gap to deterministic execution ($16.3\%$).

